Key one-line takeaway:

MCP allows an LLM such as Nemotron to use external tools such as Playwright through a standardized interface.

##############################################################################################################

Nemotron
   ↓
decides which tool
   ↓
nemotron_playwright.py
   ↓
calls MCP tool
   ↓
Playwright MCP
   ↓
Browser

##############################################################################################################

1. nemotron_playwright.py = MCP Client

Yes.

This is your Python code.

It does roughly this:

nemotron_playwright.py
        │
        ├── talks to Nemotron through OpenRouter
        │
        └── connects to Playwright MCP

In your code, this is the important part:

server_params = StdioServerParameters(
    command="playwright-mcp",
    args=[]
)

That tells your Python MCP client:

"Start the Playwright MCP server."

2. Playwright MCP = MCP Server

It is not a file that you created.

When you installed:

npm install -g @playwright/mcp

you installed the Playwright MCP server package.

The command:

playwright-mcp

starts that server.

Conceptually:

npm package
@playwright/mcp
       │
       ▼
playwright-mcp
       │
       ▼
MCP Server
       │
       ├── browser_navigate
       ├── browser_click
       ├── browser_snapshot
       ├── browser_type
       ├── browser_tabs
       └── ...

You don't normally edit the Playwright MCP source code.

3. So your complete setup is
                 ┌─────────────────────┐
                 │      Nemotron       │
                 │       LLM           │
                 └──────────┬──────────┘
                            │
                     OpenRouter API
                            │
                            ▼
                 ┌─────────────────────┐
                 │ nemotron_playwright │
                 │     .py             │
                 │                     │
                 │    MCP CLIENT       │
                 └──────────┬──────────┘
                            │
                       MCP Protocol
                            │
                            ▼
                 ┌─────────────────────┐
                 │   Playwright MCP    │
                 │     MCP SERVER      │
                 │                     │
                 │ browser_click       │
                 │ browser_navigate    │
                 │ browser_snapshot    │
                 │ etc.                │
                 └──────────┬──────────┘
                            │
                            ▼
                       Playwright
                            │
                            ▼
                         Chrome
4. Very simple analogy

Think of a restaurant:

Nemotron
   ↓
Customer
nemotron_playwright.py
   ↓
Waiter
Playwright MCP
   ↓
Kitchen / service system
Playwright + Chrome
   ↓
Actual work

The MCP Client asks the MCP Server to perform a particular tool operation.

So when Nemotron said:

browser_click

your nemotron_playwright.py received that tool call and sent it to the Playwright MCP server, which then performed the browser action.

The key distinction to remember
Component	What it is
Nemotron	LLM / AI brain
OpenRouter	API provider/gateway
nemotron_playwright.py	Your MCP Client
@playwright/mcp	Playwright MCP package/server
playwright-mcp	Command used to start the MCP server
browser_click	MCP tool provided by Playwright MCP
Playwright	Browser automation library
Chrome	Actual browser

So when we say "Nemotron is using Playwright MCP", it means:

Nemotron → your MCP Client → Playwright MCP Server → browser tool → Chrome.